<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Gregory II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">669–<a href="/years/731">731</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gregory II was pope from 715 to 731. He resisted the taxes and iconoclast policy of the Byzantine emperor Leo III, sent Boniface to convert the Germans, and in 728 received from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> king Liutprand the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutri">Sutri</a>, an act often seen as the first territorial grant to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and the beginning of the Papal States.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ii.jpg" alt="Pope Gregory II" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Śląska Szkoła Ikonograficzna · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SsigregoryIIpope.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gregory was born into a noble Roman family in 669, the son of Marcellus and Honesta. As a young man he was placed in the papal court and made a subdeacon under Pope Sergius I, later a deacon in charge of the library of the Roman Church and then a papal secretary. In 711 he accompanied Pope Constantine to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to deal with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s rejection of the canons of the Quinisext Council. Gregory handled the negotiations, and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-ii">Justinian II</a> agreed that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> could disregard whichever of the council's decisions it wished. He was consecrated on 19 May 715.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Gregory began by repairing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s Aurelian Walls, a task interrupted in October 716 when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> flooded the city for eight days. He took a close interest in missions. In 716 Duke Theodo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> visited him, and Gregory sent delegates to help establish a church hierarchy there. In 718 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Winfrid</a> approached him; he took the name Boniface and was commissioned in May 719 to preach in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Gregory made him a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in November 722. He also restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a> in 718.</p><p>He tried to keep on good terms with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> king Liutprand. Around 721 he appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> to drive the Lombards out, but Charles did not respond. Gregory and Liutprand later met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutri">Sutri</a> and reached the agreement known as the Donation of Sutri, by which Sutri and some hill towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a> were given to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, the first extension of papal territory beyond the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>From about 722 Gregory was at odds with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-iii-the-isaurian">Emperor Leo III</a>, who tried to raise taxes on the papal patrimonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to pay for an ongoing war. Gregory refused, and the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> drove out the imperial governor. When Leo issued an iconoclast edict in 726, Gregory rejected it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exarchate-of-ravenna">Exarchate of Ravenna</a> rose in revolt; Gregory dissuaded the armies from marching on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Greek sources say he excommunicated Leo, but no western source confirms it.</p><p>Gregory died on 11 February 731 and was buried in St Peter's Basilica. His defiance of Leo prepared the way for the temporal power of the popes, and he is commemorated as a saint.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Gregory II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 728 the Lombard king Liutprand handed the town of Sutri to <b>Pope Gregory II</b>, an act often seen as the first territorial grant to the papacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Gregory II</b> sent gifts to celebrate the victory.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-9">9 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=709" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Gregory II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=709">What was here in 709 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
